1. Scope and definitions

"Software" means the original Scheduler code, documentation, SDKs, scripts,
interfaces, and other materials provided under this license, including covered
portions of modified versions. It covers only materials that the relevant
copyright holders have authority to license. Third-party materials are governed
by Section 7.

"Author" means Yury Kremnev, identified above.

"Official Service" means the Scheduler service operated and controlled by the
Author, including its bot, website, administrative application, integrations,
and supporting infrastructure. Multiple components or processes operated for
that service do not constitute independent services.

"Local Development" means inspecting, modifying, building, and testing the
Software on computers under your control solely to investigate issues in the
official project or prepare, review, and propose contributions to it. Test
instances must be isolated from public access and may not serve as an actual
scheduling service for you or other end users. Operating a personal schedule
bot is not Local Development merely because it runs on your computer.

"Contribution" means code, a parser, an integration, documentation, a patch, or
other material intentionally submitted for inclusion in the official project.

2. Permissions for review and contribution

Subject to this license, you may without payment or individual approval:

(a) read and inspect the Software, including its core, and keep local copies
    for study and Local Development;

(b) modify, compile, and run local test copies only for Local Development,
    including developing and testing proposed parsers and integrations;

(c) submit proposals through issues and pull requests in the official
    repository, and share the source patches and branches necessary for their
    review through that contribution workflow;

(d) create and maintain source forks on GitHub and exercise the separate
    platform rights preserved in Section 6. A source fork is not permission
    to operate an independent service or distribute an operational release.

You may prepare and test a proposal before opening a pull request. Keep this
license and the applicable copyright and attribution notices in copies and
submitted changes. Clearly identify changed files and proposed versions. Do
not present a proposal as an official or approved release, and do not grant
recipients rights to the Software beyond those you possess.

3. No independent operational use

Except for the Author's rights and the permissions expressly preserved in this
license, no permission is granted to:

(a) run the Software as a personal, household, educational, organizational,
    internal, or commercial scheduling tool or service, even locally;

(b) deploy or make available a public, private, hosted, or self-hosted instance,
    including a separate bot, website, administrative application, or service;

(c) sell, rent, sublicense, or offer the Software as a product or service, or
    distribute releases, executable builds, container images, installation
    packages, or source copies for independent operational use;

(d) incorporate covered Software into another operational product or service,
    or use a modification or parser as a way to operate an independent copy.

These restrictions apply to unchanged and modified copies alike, whether use
is paid or free, commercial or noncommercial. Local test builds are allowed
only for the purpose stated in Section 2. Public source forks and contribution
patches remain subject to Sections 2 and 6.

